/*
Time complexity

O(log |n|)
One loop iteration per binary digit of n

Space complexity

O(1)
Only constant extra variables used

Simple intuition

Writing n in binary lets us build xⁿ using squaring

If the current bit of n is 1, multiply the result by current x

Square x each step and shift n right

Negative powers → compute normally, then take reciprocal

Convert n to long to avoid overflow (Integer.MIN_VALUE)

Why each line matters (quick sanity)

power & 1 → checks if the current bit contributes

x *= x → move to the next power of two

power >>= 1 → consume one bit

long power → prevents overflow when n = -2³¹
*/
class Solution {
    public double myPow(double x, int n) {
        if(x==0)return 0;
        if(n==0)return 1;
        double res = 1;
        long power = Math.abs((long)n);
        while(power > 0){
            if((power & 1) == 1){
                res *= x;
            }
            x*=x;
            power >>= 1;
        }
        return n>=0 ? res:(1/res);
    }
}
